{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789920578,"nonce":"xvZoE4Y8fN+LAhjlNUhHcNeVsIs=","title":"OSR: a loop with a call in it never enters the LLVM tier from a running frame","message":"The LLVM tier makes no resume helper for a loop header whose body reaches a call (Function, Symbol, Indirect, malloc, CreateClosure) or whose live-ins include a structured pointer (llvm_jit_backend.rs, install_via_mcjit: \"effectful call\", \"structured pointer live-in\"). A frame already in the loop can therefore only move to the Cranelift baseline. A function entered once with its hot loop inside it, the shape of every benchmark's `main`, runs that loop on Cranelift for the whole program unless every call in it inlines away: method_call's chunk loop, mandelbrot's run, op_overload's main all stay at the baseline (\"FIRST TRANSFER ... (cranelift)\" in ZYNTAX_OSR_TRACE). collatz reaches LLVM only because its loop has no call left.\n\nThe baseline's resume points work across calls and table pointers; the LLVM helper needs the same: live-in reconstruction for pointer-typed values, and resumption across calls and allocations, or the restriction narrowed to what is really unsafe.","files":null}]}